1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,875
When Singapore Led the
World in Canned Pineapple

2
00:00:03,875 --> 00:00:07,741
In 1912, ships leaving
Singapore carried more than half

3
00:00:07,821 --> 00:00:11,616
a million cases of canned
pineapple, and for the first

4
00:00:11,696 --> 00:00:15,849
decades of the twentieth century
the island was the largest

5
00:00:15,929 --> 00:00:19,875
producer and exporter of
canned pineapple in the world.

6
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:24,191
The fruit is almost absent from
the way Singapore tells its own

7
00:00:24,271 --> 00:00:29,225
economic story now, which tends to
start with rubber, tin and the port.

8
00:00:29,225 --> 00:00:31,800
Yet pineapple came first.

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,909
It filled some of the
island's earliest factories,

10
00:00:34,989 --> 00:00:38,735
and it seeded at least three
of the largest Chinese business

11
00:00:38,815 --> 00:00:42,816
fortunes in the region — fortunes
whose names are still attached

12
00:00:42,896 --> 00:00:45,686
to a town, a run of
schools and universities,

13
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:49,975
and a charitable foundation that
is still giving money away today.

14
00:00:49,975 --> 00:00:54,201
Pineapple reached commercial
scale in Malaya in the 1880s,

15
00:00:54,281 --> 00:00:57,250
and it took hold for a practical reason.

16
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:01,681
A rubber tree yields nothing for
its first five to seven years,

17
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:05,700
which is a long time to carry
an estate with no income.

18
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:10,239
Pineapple fruits in about eighteen
months, needs little capital,

19
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:13,350
and grows in the same poor lateritic soil.

20
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:16,325
Planters took to setting
the two out together,

21
00:01:16,405 --> 00:01:20,775
with rows of pineapple between the
young rubber, the fruit covering

22
00:01:20,855 --> 00:01:24,375
the estate's costs until
the trees were ready to tap.

23
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:27,552
Historians of the industry
call it catch-cropping,

24
00:01:27,632 --> 00:01:30,808
and it made pineapple less
a crop in its own right

25
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:34,275
than the thing that made
rubber affordable to plant.

26
00:01:34,275 --> 00:01:37,725
The canneries followed the plantations.

27
00:01:37,725 --> 00:01:42,954
Pineapple canning became one of
Singapore's first manufacturing industries,

28
00:01:43,034 --> 00:01:46,423
clustered along the
Singapore River around Clarke

29
00:01:46,503 --> 00:01:50,750
Quay and later spreading to
Sembawang, Rochor and Serangoon.

30
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:53,575
Some were large operations.

31
00:01:53,575 --> 00:01:58,106
One cannery run by a
Corsican-Italian named J. P. Bastiani

32
00:01:58,186 --> 00:02:02,400
was turning out five thousand
cans a day at its peak.

33
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:05,184
Most were Chinese-owned, and a handful

34
00:02:05,264 --> 00:02:07,600
of the owners became very rich.

35
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:12,660
Local lore later grouped four of them
together as the "pineapple kings":

36
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:16,944
Tan Tye, the earliest, who
arrived in 1860 and ran canneries

37
00:02:17,024 --> 00:02:21,450
at Clarke Quay; and the three
whose names outlasted the fruit.

38
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:25,585
The name a Singaporean is
most likely to half-recognise,

39
00:02:25,665 --> 00:02:28,450
without knowing why, is Lim Nee Soon.

40
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:33,925
He was born in Singapore in 1879 to a
Teochew family with roots in Shantou,

41
00:02:34,005 --> 00:02:37,480
founded his own firm in
1911, and built up large

42
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:41,850
estates in the north of the
island — Sembawang, Chong Pang,

43
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:46,300
Bah Soon Pah, and the district
that came to carry his name.

44
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,365
He planted rubber and
pineapple across them,

45
00:02:49,445 --> 00:02:54,725
advised other estate owners, ran a
transport business, and served two terms

46
00:02:54,805 --> 00:02:58,950
as president of the Singapore
Chinese Chamber of Commerce.

47
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:02,525
The newspapers called
him the "Pineapple King."

48
00:03:02,525 --> 00:03:06,784
He died in Shanghai in 1936
and was buried in Nanjing,

49
00:03:06,864 --> 00:03:10,882
near Sun Yat-sen's mausoleum,
a measure of how deep

50
00:03:10,962 --> 00:03:14,900
his ties to the Chinese
nationalist movement ran.

51
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,250
His estates became Yishun New Town.

52
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:22,503
"Nee Soon" and "Yishun" are the
same two Chinese characters:

53
00:03:22,583 --> 00:03:24,814
the first is the Teochew reading

54
00:03:24,894 --> 00:03:28,000
of his given name, the
second the Mandarin.

55
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,816
Nee Soon Road was gazetted in 1950,

56
00:03:30,896 --> 00:03:34,950
and the district kept the
older form for decades.

57
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:39,094
When Singapore standardised
place names to their Mandarin

58
00:03:39,174 --> 00:03:42,873
readings in the 1980s, in
the years after the Speak

59
00:03:42,953 --> 00:03:47,325
Mandarin Campaign began, Nee
Soon on the map became Yishun.

60
00:03:47,325 --> 00:03:50,616
The change drew public
complaints at the time,

61
00:03:50,696 --> 00:03:53,700
and the older name never fully went away.

62
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:57,200
It survives on Nee Soon
Road, at Nee Soon Camp,

63
00:03:57,280 --> 00:04:00,250
and in the Nee Soon electoral division.

64
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:05,323
I spent most of my national service at
Khatib Camp, a few minutes from there,

65
00:04:05,403 --> 00:04:08,950
and on weekends we would go
into Nee Soon for a meal.

66
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:11,381
It never once occurred to me that

67
00:04:11,461 --> 00:04:14,125
the place was named after a person.

68
00:04:14,125 --> 00:04:17,348
The largest of the
pineapple fortunes belonged

69
00:04:17,428 --> 00:04:20,875
to Tan Kah Kee, and
pineapple is where it began.

70
00:04:20,875 --> 00:04:24,383
He came from Fujian in 1890 and joined his

71
00:04:24,463 --> 00:04:28,650
father's rice business,
which failed around 1904.

72
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:32,107
With a few thousand Straits
dollars he opened a small

73
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:36,043
pineapple cannery in Sembawang,
then a second, then a third

74
00:04:36,123 --> 00:04:40,314
by the Rochor River, and used the
proceeds to move into rubber —

75
00:04:40,394 --> 00:04:43,383
first plantations, then
the manufacturing that

76
00:04:43,463 --> 00:04:47,000
made him one of the wealthiest
men in Southeast Asia.

77
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,557
By the mid-1920s his companies ran
some fifteen thousand acres of rubber,

78
00:04:52,637 --> 00:04:57,425
dozens of factories, and a
workforce in the tens of thousands.

79
00:04:57,425 --> 00:04:59,425
Then it came apart.

80
00:04:59,425 --> 00:05:03,976
The Depression pushed rubber and
pineapple prices down together,

81
00:05:04,056 --> 00:05:07,087
his businesses could
not carry their debts,

82
00:05:07,167 --> 00:05:09,700
and in 1934 the group was wound up.

83
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:12,130
He is remembered instead for what he

84
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:14,650
did with the money while he had it.

85
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:19,937
He founded The Chinese High School
in Singapore in 1919 and Xiamen

86
00:05:20,017 --> 00:05:25,141
University in China in 1921, and
he funded schools across Fujian

87
00:05:25,221 --> 00:05:30,425
and the diaspora, by one count
more than a hundred institutions.

88
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:34,436
During the war he led the
overseas Chinese fundraising

89
00:05:34,516 --> 00:05:38,830
drive against Japan, which put
him high on the Kempeitai's

90
00:05:38,910 --> 00:05:42,850
wanted list and forced him
out of Singapore in 1942.

91
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:47,083
He left for China for good
in 1950, took a role in

92
00:05:47,162 --> 00:05:51,475
the government there, and
died in Beijing in 1961.

93
00:05:51,475 --> 00:05:55,400
The third fortune grew
straight out of the second.

94
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:59,275
Lee Kong Chian arrived
in Singapore from Fujian

95
00:05:59,355 --> 00:06:03,229
as a boy in 1903, went
to work for Tan Kah Kee,

96
00:06:03,309 --> 00:06:06,675
and in 1920 married his eldest daughter.

97
00:06:06,675 --> 00:06:11,430
He then built his own group: Lee
Rubber, founded in the late 1920s,

98
00:06:11,510 --> 00:06:14,172
which became one of the largest rubber

99
00:06:14,252 --> 00:06:18,573
businesses in the region, and
Lee Pineapple, which he and his

100
00:06:18,653 --> 00:06:22,550
brother assembled from
existing canneries around 1930.

101
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,280
He later chaired OCBC
and served as the first

102
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:30,000
pro-chancellor of the
University of Malaya.

103
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,950
Lee Pineapple is the reason any
of this is more than history.

104
00:06:34,950 --> 00:06:38,744
As the plantations and canneries
left Singapore for Johor

105
00:06:38,824 --> 00:06:42,685
after the war, the company
went with them and kept canning

106
00:06:42,765 --> 00:06:46,775
pineapple at a factory in
Skudai, just across the Causeway.

107
00:06:46,775 --> 00:06:49,925
It ran there for ninety-two years.

108
00:06:49,925 --> 00:06:54,026
Only at the end of 2023 did
it stop canning pineapple and

109
00:06:54,106 --> 00:06:57,839
turn the land over to oil
palm, which is, fittingly,

110
00:06:57,919 --> 00:07:02,100
the crop that displaced
pineapple almost everywhere else.

111
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:07,224
Lee also set up the Lee Foundation
in 1952, and it remains one

112
00:07:07,304 --> 00:07:12,675
of Singapore's largest private
funders of education and welfare.

113
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:17,738
His name is on the National Library's
reference library, on a medical school,

114
00:07:17,818 --> 00:07:21,425
on a natural history museum,
and on a business school.

115
00:07:21,425 --> 00:07:24,009
Singapore's part of the industry did

116
00:07:24,089 --> 00:07:26,975
not so much collapse as get priced out.

117
00:07:26,975 --> 00:07:30,291
Pineapple is a land-hungry
crop with thin margins,

118
00:07:30,371 --> 00:07:33,824
and on an island where land
was about to become some

119
00:07:33,904 --> 00:07:37,560
of the most valuable in the
world, an acre of pineapple

120
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:40,900
could not compete with
an acre of anything else.

121
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:46,048
The plantations were subdivided and
built over from the 1950s onward,

122
00:07:46,128 --> 00:07:50,518
the canneries followed the
fruit north to Johor, and by the

123
00:07:50,598 --> 00:07:55,750
1960s there was no pineapple industry
left in Singapore to speak of.

124
00:07:55,750 --> 00:07:59,408
Malaya's own canning industry
peaked in the late 1960s,

125
00:07:59,488 --> 00:08:04,574
when the country was the world's
third-largest exporter of canned pineapple,

126
00:08:04,654 --> 00:08:07,293
and then fell away in turn as rubber and

127
00:08:07,373 --> 00:08:11,371
oil palm drew off the land and
the capital, and as Thailand,

128
00:08:11,451 --> 00:08:15,325
the Philippines and Indonesia
took over the world market.

129
00:08:15,325 --> 00:08:17,670
Where it fits in the bigger story:

130
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:22,807
The pineapple industry is a rare case
of something genuinely large being

131
00:08:22,887 --> 00:08:26,525
written almost entirely
out of the national memory.

132
00:08:26,525 --> 00:08:31,006
There is no monument to it, no
museum on this side of the Causeway,

133
00:08:31,086 --> 00:08:34,150
nothing on the map that
reads as "pineapple."

134
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:37,755
What is left instead is
indirect: the name of a town that

135
00:08:37,835 --> 00:08:40,277
most people no longer connect to a man,

136
00:08:40,357 --> 00:08:44,479
a foundation still signing cheques
seventy years on, and a tin of

137
00:08:44,559 --> 00:08:48,164
pineapple tarts that comes
out once a year without anyone

138
00:08:48,244 --> 00:08:51,800
thinking about where the
fruit in it used to come from.
